// Vitest Snapshot v1, https://vitest.dev/guide/snapshot.html

exports[`pins the multiplayer tool declarations and instructions connected agents read > connected instructions with multiplayer 1`] = `
"wasd_export_web builds the Godot project for the Web into its .wasdengine/web folder, wasd_export_desktop builds it for Windows and macOS into its .wasdengine/desktop folder, and wasd_export_mobile builds it for Android and iOS into its .wasdengine/mobile folder. wasd_record_preview records the game's preview, a short animation, a GIF, and a still of the running game, which Explore, the game's page, and link previews of the share link show: start the game with godot_run, bring it to its gameplay, then record with events that play it. wasd_release_game builds the project and publishes the build as the game's next numbered version on WASD Engine with the recorded preview, and returns the share link, where anyone with the link can play the game, pick earlier versions, and comment; visibility public also lists the game on Explore, private keeps it off, and desktop adds Windows and macOS downloads. The game's page credits what made the game: pass model, the full name of the AI model you are, such as Claude Opus 5.5, with every release, and it lists the game tools and add-ons that worked on the project. Release only when the user asks to, pass notes that say what changed, and on a game's first release pass description, what the game is and how to play it. Release the game as it is: wasd_release_game first checks the Web build in a browser and publishes nothing when the game does not start, crashes, stops answering, or prints errors there; then tell the user what the check found, and change the game only when they ask. Releasing needs WASD Engine to be signed in.

Multiplayer: a game goes online player-hosted, free, where one player's browser hosts and friends join with a code, or on a dedicated server that WASD Engine runs for a monthly price by size; players sign in with a WASD Engine account in both. Call wasd_multiplayer_guide first and show your user the comparison with its costs before wasd_multiplayer_setup. State the monthly price and what it includes before suggesting a server, or that it is free while one is left when your user confirms, if wasd_server_plans lists free servers of that size left for your user's account, and let your user choose the size from wasd_server_plans; never pick one. wasd_server_create and wasd_server_change return a page where your user confirms on the WASD Engine site, and nothing is charged before that: give them the page. Explain that the owner gets emails at 70%, 90%, and 100% of a server's monthly bandwidth and that at 100% the server stops until its next billing month. Pass confirm: true to wasd_server_manage restore only after your user agreed to replace the world."
`;

exports[`pins the multiplayer tool declarations and instructions connected agents read > connected multiplayer tool declarations 1`] = `
[
  {
    "description": "Explain the two ways a Godot game goes online with WASD Engine, with the live server sizes and prices, and recommend one for this game. Player-hosted is free: one player's browser hosts and friends join with a code. A dedicated server runs the game's server build around the clock for a monthly price by size. Players sign in with a WASD Engine account in both. Call it before setting up multiplayer, and show the user its for_user text so they choose.",
    "inputSchema": {
      "additionalProperties": false,
      "properties": {
        "competitive": {
          "description": "true for ranked or competitive play, where cheating matters.",
          "type": "boolean",
        },
        "genre": {
          "description": "What kind of game it is, such as "co-op shooter" or "survival sandbox".",
          "type": "string",
        },
        "persistent_world": {
          "description": "true when the world must keep going while nobody plays.",
          "type": "boolean",
        },
        "players": {
          "description": "Most players the game should have together.",
          "type": "integer",
        },
        "project": {
          "description": "Absolute path of the folder that holds project.godot.",
          "type": "string",
        },
      },
      "required": [],
      "type": "object",
    },
    "name": "wasd_multiplayer_guide",
  },
  {
    "description": "Set the Godot project up for online multiplayer in the mode the user chose: copy the WASD Multiplayer add-on into addons/wasd_multiplayer, turn on its WasdMultiplayer autoload and editor plugin, and set the mode, the most players, the site, and the released game in res://wasd_multiplayer.cfg, keeping the file's other settings. The add-on handles WASD sign-in, lobbies, connections, and dedicated server joins; the game uses Godot's own multiplayer API (@rpc, MultiplayerSpawner, MultiplayerSynchronizer). Calling it again switches the mode or updates the add-on: it replaces the add-on's own files, first keeping the ones the game changed in a backup folder that the result names, and keeps the example files the game changed and the files it added.",
    "inputSchema": {
      "additionalProperties": false,
      "properties": {
        "max_players": {
          "description": "Most players together. Default: 8 for player_hosted (at most 16), 32 for dedicated.",
          "type": "integer",
        },
        "mode": {
          "description": "player_hosted (free, a player hosts) or dedicated (a server WASD Engine runs).",
          "enum": [
            "player_hosted",
            "dedicated",
          ],
          "type": "string",
        },
        "project": {
          "description": "Absolute path of the folder that holds project.godot.",
          "type": "string",
        },
      },
      "required": [
        "project",
        "mode",
      ],
      "type": "object",
    },
    "name": "wasd_multiplayer_setup",
  },
  {
    "description": "Test the game's multiplayer on this computer: run the host, or the dedicated server, and 1 to 3 clients as headless instances that connect over loopback, with made-up players and no WASD Engine services or costs. Reports whether every client connected, the server's frame time, memory, processor use, and traffic per player-hour, and which server size holds the game. Needs wasd_multiplayer_setup first; the game's main scene must use WasdMultiplayer.",
    "inputSchema": {
      "additionalProperties": false,
      "properties": {
        "clients": {
          "description": "Clients to connect, 1 to 3. Default: 2.",
          "type": "integer",
        },
        "mode": {
          "description": "Mode to test. Default: the mode the project is set up for.",
          "enum": [
            "player_hosted",
            "dedicated",
          ],
          "type": "string",
        },
        "project": {
          "description": "Absolute path of the folder that holds project.godot.",
          "type": "string",
        },
        "seconds": {
          "description": "How long the host or server runs, 10 to 120 seconds. Default: 30.",
          "type": "integer",
        },
      },
      "required": [
        "project",
      ],
      "type": "object",
    },
    "name": "wasd_multiplayer_test",
  },
  {
    "description": "Show the released game's player-hosted lobbies: how many are open, how many players are in them, and this month's relay traffic against the fair-use level, past which the relay is off for the game until the next month. Player-hosted multiplayer is free; nothing is billed.",
    "inputSchema": {
      "additionalProperties": false,
      "properties": {
        "project": {
          "description": "Absolute path of the folder that holds project.godot.",
          "type": "string",
        },
      },
      "required": [
        "project",
      ],
      "type": "object",
    },
    "name": "wasd_lobby_stats",
  },
  {
    "description": "List the dedicated server sizes with their monthly prices, CPU, RAM, bandwidth, storage, and how many players each holds, from the WASD Engine site, and the free servers WASD Engine gave the signed-in account, which cost nothing. After wasd_multiplayer_test, it also fits each size to what the test measured. Show the user the sizes and prices and let them choose; never pick a size for them.",
    "inputSchema": {
      "additionalProperties": false,
      "properties": {
        "project": {
          "description": "Absolute path of the folder that holds project.godot.",
          "type": "string",
        },
      },
      "required": [],
      "type": "object",
    },
    "name": "wasd_server_plans",
  },
  {
    "description": "Create a dedicated server for the released game, of a size the user chose from wasd_server_plans. It never charges by itself: it returns a page on the WASD Engine site where the user confirms and pays, which needs a valid payment method on their account; a size of which wasd_server_plans lists free servers left for the account is free while one is left when the user confirms, and then needs no card. Tell the user the size's monthly price, or that it is free while one of those free servers is left, and what it includes before calling it. The server then runs the server build of the game's latest release.",
    "inputSchema": {
      "additionalProperties": false,
      "properties": {
        "name": {
          "description": "Name of the server, at most 48 characters. Default: the game's title.",
          "type": "string",
        },
        "project": {
          "description": "Absolute path of the folder that holds project.godot.",
          "type": "string",
        },
        "size": {
          "description": "Size id from wasd_server_plans, such as small.",
          "type": "string",
        },
      },
      "required": [
        "project",
        "size",
      ],
      "type": "object",
    },
    "name": "wasd_server_create",
  },
  {
    "description": "Deploy a release's server build to the game's server: the build of the game's latest release, the version players join, unless version names another, such as an earlier version to roll back; a latest release without a server build cannot be deployed. Players get a 60-second warning, then the server restarts on that build; the world is kept. Releases deploy to the game's servers by themselves, so call this for a rollback or a server that missed one. Tell the user the server restarts.",
    "inputSchema": {
      "additionalProperties": false,
      "properties": {
        "project": {
          "description": "Absolute path of the folder that holds project.godot.",
          "type": "string",
        },
        "server": {
          "description": "Server id from wasd_server_status. Default: the game's only server.",
          "type": "string",
        },
        "version": {
          "description": "Release whose server build to deploy. Default: the game's latest release.",
          "type": "integer",
        },
      },
      "required": [
        "project",
      ],
      "type": "object",
    },
    "name": "wasd_server_deploy",
  },
  {
    "description": "Show the game's dedicated servers, or one of them: state, address, release version, players, CPU and memory, this month's bandwidth and storage against the size's allowance, the last backup, and the price. A server that used all its bandwidth stops until the allowance resets with its next billing month.",
    "inputSchema": {
      "additionalProperties": false,
      "properties": {
        "project": {
          "description": "Absolute path of the folder that holds project.godot.",
          "type": "string",
        },
        "server": {
          "description": "Server id. Default: every server of the game.",
          "type": "string",
        },
      },
      "required": [
        "project",
      ],
      "type": "object",
    },
    "name": "wasd_server_status",
  },
  {
    "description": "Read the latest lines of a dedicated server's log: what the game printed, its errors, and WASD Engine's notes such as restarts.",
    "inputSchema": {
      "additionalProperties": false,
      "properties": {
        "limit": {
          "description": "Most lines, 1 to 500. Default: 200.",
          "type": "integer",
        },
        "project": {
          "description": "Absolute path of the folder that holds project.godot.",
          "type": "string",
        },
        "server": {
          "description": "Server id from wasd_server_status. Default: the game's only server.",
          "type": "string",
        },
      },
      "required": [
        "project",
      ],
      "type": "object",
    },
    "name": "wasd_server_logs",
  },
  {
    "description": "Start, stop, restart, back up, or restore a dedicated server. Stopping keeps the world and the monthly price; only deleting ends billing. Players get a warning before a stop or restart. restore replaces the current world with a backup from wasd_server_status or the backup list it returns, so ask the user first.",
    "inputSchema": {
      "additionalProperties": false,
      "properties": {
        "action": {
          "description": "backups lists the backups to restore from.",
          "enum": [
            "start",
            "stop",
            "restart",
            "backup",
            "restore",
            "backups",
          ],
          "type": "string",
        },
        "backup_id": {
          "description": "The backup to restore.",
          "type": "string",
        },
        "confirm": {
          "description": "true only once your user agreed to replace the server's world with the backup; needed for restore.",
          "type": "boolean",
        },
        "project": {
          "description": "Absolute path of the folder that holds project.godot.",
          "type": "string",
        },
        "server": {
          "description": "Server id from wasd_server_status. Default: the game's only server.",
          "type": "string",
        },
      },
      "required": [
        "project",
        "action",
      ],
      "type": "object",
    },
    "name": "wasd_server_manage",
  },
  {
    "description": "Change a dedicated server's size, or delete the server. It never changes the bill by itself: it returns a page on the WASD Engine site where the user confirms. For a paid server, a bigger size is charged from the confirmation, prorated, and a smaller one credited. A free server (wasd_server_status marks it) moved to a size without free servers left becomes a new purchase at that size's monthly price, with no credit. A size with free servers left for the account (wasd_server_plans lists them) is free while one is left when the user confirms. Deleting stops the server and deletes its world 7 days later; a paid server's monthly price comes off the bill unless the confirmation page says another of the account's servers keeps it, and a free one changes no bill. Deleting a server whose purchase still awaits confirmation cancels that purchase, which never ran or charged anything. Tell the user the price difference or what deleting loses before calling it.",
    "inputSchema": {
      "additionalProperties": false,
      "properties": {
        "delete": {
          "description": "true deletes the server.",
          "type": "boolean",
        },
        "project": {
          "description": "Absolute path of the folder that holds project.godot.",
          "type": "string",
        },
        "server": {
          "description": "Server id from wasd_server_status. Default: the game's only server.",
          "type": "string",
        },
        "size": {
          "description": "New size id from wasd_server_plans.",
          "type": "string",
        },
      },
      "required": [
        "project",
      ],
      "type": "object",
    },
    "name": "wasd_server_change",
  },
]
`;

exports[`pins the multiplayer tool declarations and the multiplayer section a Session's agent reads > multiplayer section 1`] = `
"# Multiplayer

WASD Engine puts Godot games online two ways, and players sign in with a WASD Engine account in both. Player-hosted is free: one player's browser hosts and friends join with a code, which suits co-op and party games of 2 to 8 players. A dedicated server runs the game's server build for a monthly price by size and suits persistent worlds and larger games: it sleeps while nobody plays and wakes when a player joins, and on the larger sizes its owner can keep it on (Always on) so the world keeps going.

When the user wants multiplayer:
1. Call wasd_multiplayer_guide first and show the user its comparison with the costs before setting anything up; then call wasd_multiplayer_setup with the mode they choose, and wasd_multiplayer_test once the game connects players.
2. State the monthly price and what it includes before suggesting a server; when wasd_server_plans lists free servers of a size left for the account, a server of that size is free while one is left when the user confirms, so say that instead.
3. Let the user choose the size from wasd_server_plans; never pick one for them. wasd_server_create and wasd_server_change only open a page where the user confirms on the WASD Engine site; nothing is charged before that.
4. Explain that the owner gets emails at 70%, 90%, and 100% of a server's monthly bandwidth, and that at 100% the server stops until its next billing month; suggest a smaller size when a server is mostly idle.
5. When the world must keep going while nobody plays, tell the user to turn on Always on for the server on the WASD Engine site; only the sizes wasd_server_plans names offer it."
`;

